22:18:34 | INFO | HTTP Request: POST https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
22:18:34 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=0 miss=645
22:18:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s understandable to feel that way, because scandals get far more attention than the many routine decisions politicians make ethically. But “some politicians are corrupt” does not prove that *all* politicians are corrupt deep down. A few reasons the impression is so strong: - **Bad conduct is newsworthy; ordinary integrity usually isn’t.** “Official followed disclosure rules and declined a favor” rarely becomes a headline. - **Politics creates unusually visible conflicts and scrutiny.** A mistake, poor judgment call, or real abuse can become national news in a way that similar conduct in many other professions may not. - **The incentives can be genuinely dangerous.** Power, money, ambition, partisan loyalty, and access to donors or lobbyists can make ethical compromise easier. That is a reason for safeguards—not proof that everyone inevitably fails. - **Scandals are not all the same.** Some involve criminal bribery or self-dealing; others involve careless wording, undisclosed relationships, campaign-rule errors, or conduct that is unethical but not illegal. Accountability matters in all cases, but they should not be treated as identical. Your cousin should be neither cynical nor naïve. She can assume that the job will put pressure on her integrity, then build systems that make wrongdoing harder and accountability easier: transparent finances, recusal rules, independent ethics counsel, public explanations for decisions, and people around her who are willing to say “no.” Integrity in politics is not demonstrated by claiming to be perfect. It is demonstrated by being open about conflicts, correcting mistakes promptly, accepting scrutiny, and refusing to turn power into a private benefit.
