22:18:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s a common belief, but common belief is not the same as proof. People tend to remember the politician who abuses power, not the one who spends years doing constituent casework, negotiating budgets, or voting on unglamorous local issues without a scandal. There is also a real kernel behind the cynicism: political office can attract ambitious people and create temptations around status, money, donors, and influence. So your cousin should not assume good character alone will protect her. But it does not follow that self-interest is the only motive, or that corruption is inevitable. A more useful stance is: - **Assume incentives can distort judgment.** - **Build rules that expose and limit conflicts.** - **Make decisions explainable in public.** - **Keep independent sources of feedback and accountability.** - **Leave or speak up if an institution demands unethical conduct.** People may still stereotype her because of the profession. The strongest answer is a consistent record over time: transparent disclosures, principled recusals, honest explanations, and a willingness to take politically inconvenient positions when the public interest requires it.
