22:19:00 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=0 miss=1278
22:19:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That saying endures because it captures a real danger: power can reduce consequences, increase temptation, surround someone with flatterers, and make it easier to rationalize selfish choices as “necessary.” It is a useful warning, especially for anyone entering public life. But it is not a proven law of human nature. Power does not mechanically turn every person corrupt. It can also reveal character, magnify existing habits, and create opportunities to do substantial good. The important question is not whether she is somehow immune, but whether she will treat power as something that requires limits. A healthy principle for her might be: > “The more authority I have, the less I should rely on my own good intentions alone.” That means embracing checks even when they are inconvenient: independent ethics review, public disclosures, clear recusal practices, transparent fundraising, term limits or self-imposed boundaries where appropriate, and advisers who can disagree without being punished. She should also watch for the classic early warning signs: feeling entitled to special treatment, treating critics as enemies rather than potential correctives, believing rules are for lesser people, or convincing herself that an unethical act is justified because her goals are good. Those are often more dangerous than obvious villainy. So the proverb should make her cautious and humble—not persuade her that integrity is futile. A person who expects power to test them is better positioned to resist its worst effects.
