22:19:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Neither. Distrust can be rational without implying that every politician—or even most individual politicians—is personally corrupt. Millions of voters may reasonably see: - repeated scandals and broken promises; - lobbying and donor influence; - revolving-door jobs between government and industry; - opaque budgeting and deal-making; - partisan incentives that reward loyalty over honesty; - weak consequences for misconduct. Those patterns justify skepticism and demands for stronger safeguards. But they do not establish that all politicians share the same motives or behavior. Public distrust can also be shaped by selective news coverage, partisan messaging, and the fact that failures are much more visible than routine ethical conduct. A fair conclusion is: **the political environment often gives people good reason to distrust unchecked power.** The answer is not blind faith in politicians, nor blanket condemnation of every person in office; it is transparency, enforceable rules, independent oversight, and voters who judge officials by evidence.
