22:19:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: “Follow the money” is often a very good investigative rule. Campaign funding can buy access, shape which issues get attention, create dependency, and make politicians reluctant to cross powerful interests. Even when no explicit quid pro quo exists, that influence can distort representation. But funding does not literally control every vote. Politicians also respond to constituents, party coalitions, ideology, public opinion, media scrutiny, unions, advocacy groups, courts, and their own judgment. The relationship is often influence and incentive—not a simple ownership contract. For your cousin, the practical implication is still serious: she should make campaign money one of her strongest ethics controls. - Prefer small donors, public financing, or grassroots funding where possible. - Disclose donations promptly and clearly. - Do not promise policy outcomes, meetings, or special access in exchange for contributions. - Separate fundraising from official decision-making as much as the rules allow. - Publish a policy explaining how she handles donor meetings and conflicts. - Refuse donations that create obvious conflicts or dependence, even if legal. - Support stronger disclosure, lobbying, procurement, and campaign-finance rules—including rules that would constrain her own campaign. The public is right to be wary when money and power mix. The meaningful distinction is whether an official treats donors as citizens with views, or as customers purchasing government action.
