22:20:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A well-researched documentary may identify real structural problems: lobbying access, donor dependence, weak disclosure, regulatory capture, and the revolving door between public office and private industry are all legitimate concerns. Investigations have documented cases where those dynamics led to improper influence or outright corruption. But even if a system is skewed or vulnerable to capture, that does not mean every person within it is knowingly corrupt. Systems can reward behavior that is legal yet harmful, and they can pressure decent people toward compromises. That is exactly why reform matters. Your cousin could decide that the ethical path is not to pretend the system is clean, but to enter with clear limits and push for reforms such as: - stronger, real-time campaign-finance disclosure; - public financing or small-donor matching; - longer cooling-off periods before officials and senior staff can lobby or join regulated industries; - transparent lobbying logs and meeting calendars; - independent ethics enforcement with meaningful penalties; - clearer recusal and procurement rules; - public access to legislative text and amendments before votes. She should also be honest with herself: if the office or party organization requires conduct she considers unethical, she should be prepared to oppose it publicly, resign, or not run. Integrity sometimes means declining power rather than accepting it on corrupting terms.
