22:21:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You don’t have to become an apologist to reject an absolute claim. A non-apologist position can be: > Politics often concentrates power and money in ways that predictably produce corruption, conflicts of interest, and public betrayal. Politicians therefore deserve skepticism, close scrutiny, and strong limits—not automatic trust. That is much tougher than “most politicians are trying their best,” while still avoiding “every single one is corrupt.” The latter can unintentionally let genuinely corrupt officials off the hook too: if corruption is assumed to be universal and unavoidable, then no particular act stands out as a betrayal and reform can seem pointless. It is reasonable to judge politicians by their records, especially on concrete indicators: who funds them, whom they meet with, whether they disclose conflicts, whether they profit from office, whether they support oversight that applies to themselves, and whether their votes consistently favor narrow powerful interests over constituents. Your cousin should understand that she will not get trust merely by asking for it or claiming good motives. She should expect to have to earn it through conduct that can be checked publicly—and accept that people are right to remain skeptical.
