22:22:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is a legitimate concern. She should not enter politics assuming everyone is decent, nor assuming everyone is corrupt. Either assumption can impair judgment. A better operating stance is **trust principles and evidence, not status or charm**: - Assume conflicts of interest are common and verify claims. - Treat gifts, donor access, favors, and job offers as potential leverage—not harmless generosity. - Keep important decisions documented and avoid off-the-record arrangements. - Get independent legal and ethics advice before acting in gray areas. - Separate campaign activity from official duties. - Build relationships with people who have a record of integrity, but still maintain safeguards. - Learn how influence actually works in her jurisdiction: procurement, lobbying, party discipline, fundraising, appointments, and post-office employment. - Set clear red lines in advance, including when she will refuse, disclose, report, or walk away. - Support rules that apply equally to allies and opponents. She can be skeptical without becoming cynical. Cynicism says corruption is inevitable, so there is no point resisting it. Skepticism says corruption is possible and sometimes likely, so verify, disclose, document, and enforce consequences. That mindset is more likely to protect her from being used—and to help her remain trustworthy herself.
