22:23:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target: There is a real human tendency there: power, status, money, and low accountability can encourage entitlement, favoritism, and self-serving rationalization. Treating that risk as the default is prudent. But “natural” does not mean unavoidable or unchangeable. Much of civilized life depends on designing institutions around predictable human weaknesses: courts, contracts, accounting, separation of powers, inspections, elections, and professional standards are all, in a sense, “artificial patches.” That is not a flaw—it is the point of institutions. For your cousin, the useful lesson is not “good intentions will be enough.” It is: - Expect temptation and pressure. - Avoid situations where private benefit and public duty overlap. - Make actions visible and reviewable. - Give others real power to stop or investigate misconduct. - Accept rules that constrain her even when she believes she is acting well. - Stay willing to give up influence rather than compromise a core principle. She does not need to deny human nature to maintain integrity. She needs to take it seriously enough to build protections against it—including protections against her own blind spots.
