22:26:19 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I understand the appeal. A simple rule can make repeated disappointments, scandals, and obvious double standards feel easier to interpret—and it can protect you from being naïve. The downside is that it can turn healthy skepticism into resignation: if corruption is assumed to be universal, then evidence, accountability, and reform can start to feel pointless. A more protective rule that keeps the clarity is: > **Assume public officials have incentives and opportunities to serve themselves; require evidence and oversight before trusting them.** That still makes room for the ugly realities you’re pointing to, without requiring you to pretend every case is identical. It also gives your cousin a practical standard: she should expect distrust, make her conduct transparent, and accept scrutiny rather than asking people to believe she is different.
